To develop a phpz extension, install a Zig toolchain and a PHP development environment that meet these requirements:

| Component | Supported configuration |
| --- | --- |
| Zig | **0.17.0** |
| PHP | **8.2–8.6**, NTS and ZTS |
| Linux | x86_64, aarch64 |
| macOS | x86_64 (Intel), aarch64 (Apple Silicon) |
| Windows | x64, MSVC ABI |

# [Zig toolchain]($heading.id('zig-toolchain'))

The repository and generated skeleton require Zig `0.17.0` or later and use `translate-c 2.0.2` to generate C bindings.

Install the toolchain for your OS and architecture, add its directory to `PATH`, and check the full version with `zig version`. Whether you use a system package or a [Zig download](https://ziglang.org/download/), compare the full version against the project manifest. Development snapshots with the same version prefix can differ.

# [PHP development environment]($heading.id('php-development-environment'))

Install PHP with your package manager or build it from source. Extension development requires the PHP CLI, the tokenizer extension and matching development headers. Match the PHP version, architecture, thread-safety mode (NTS/ZTS) and debug configuration across the runtime and development files.

## [Install using a package manager]($heading.id('install-using-a-package-manager'))

Choose the commands for your platform:

```sh
# Arch Linux
pacman -S php
# Debian / Ubuntu
apt install php-cli php-dev
# Fedora
dnf install php-cli php-devel
# macOS (Homebrew)
brew install php
```

On Linux and macOS, verify that `php` and `php-config` are on your path. Use `php-config --include-dir` to locate the headers.

## [Windows]($heading.id('windows'))

On Windows, download the **Development package** matching your runtime from the [PHP downloads page](https://www.php.net/downloads.php?os=windows). It contains the headers and import library.

See [Building with MSVC on Windows]($link.page('development/windows').ref('windows-msvc')) for development package configuration and build instructions.

## [Build PHP from source]($heading.id('build-php-from-source'))

For extension development, a **debug build of PHP** provides debug symbols, Zend assertions and memory leak reports. Configure PHP with `--enable-debug` for development and testing. Use a release build for performance measurements.

For the Linux and macOS build process, see [Building PHP](https://www.phpinternalsbook.com/php7/build_system/building_php.html) in the PHP Internals Book. Select a PHP version in the supported range above.

After installing the compiler and development dependencies, use the following commands to build a debug PHP from the PHP 8.6 branch and install it under your home directory:

```sh
# Clone PHP and check out the release branch
git clone https://github.com/php/php-src.git
cd php-src
git checkout PHP-8.6
./buildconf

PHP_PREFIX="${HOME}/build/php"
./configure --prefix="${PHP_PREFIX}" \
    --disable-all \
    --enable-tokenizer \
    --enable-debug
make -j
make install
export PATH="${PHP_PREFIX}/bin:$PATH"
php -v
php-config --include-dir
```

For a thread-safe build, add `--enable-zts` to `./configure`.

This `PATH` setting applies to the current shell.

# [Next steps]($heading.id('next-steps'))

Once the environment is ready, continue with [Your first PHP extension]($link.page('getting-started/hello-world')).
